In this lesson we'll look at how to tell rational numbers apart from irrational ones. Every real number, meaning anything you can place on a number line, falls into one of two big families. Let's start with rationals. A number is rational if you can write it as a fraction of two integers, where the bottom one isn't zero. That sounds restrictive, but it actually covers a lot. Every whole number counts, since you can always rewrite it over one, like five equals five over one. It also covers decimals that stop, like zero point seven five, and decimals that repeat forever, like zero point three three three and so on, which equals one third. Then come the irrationals. These are numbers that simply cannot be written as a fraction of integers, and the giveaway is that their decimals go on forever without ever repeating. Famous examples are the square root of two, pi, the square root of three, and the golden ratio. Here's a handy shortcut. The square root of a perfect square, like four, nine, or sixteen, is rational because it comes out as a tidy whole number. But the square root of any non-perfect square, like two, three, five, or seven, is irrational. Now let's see it in action.